course: geography
lesson: 49
title: Sedimentary and metamorphic rocks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sedimentary rocks are made of bits: sand, mud, pebbles or shells settle in layers, get buried under more layers, and are pressed and cemented into stone, like a stack of pancakes squashed into one cake.
explain: Because each layer settles on top of the one before, in rock that has not been disturbed the lowest layers are the oldest, so a cliff face reads like a history book from the bottom up.
explain: Walk along the white cliffs of Dover and pick up a piece of chalk: it is made of the shells of countless tiny sea creatures that lived in a warm sea about 90 million years ago.
explain: Metamorphic rocks are rocks changed by heat and pressure without melting, the way clay in a kiln turns into a hard pot: limestone becomes marble, and shale becomes slate.
explain: When plates collide, layers of rock can be bent into folds like a rug pushed against a wall, and old towns built from local stone show the geology under their streets.
fact: Sedimentary rocks form when layers of sand, mud, shells or other bits are buried, pressed and cemented together.
fact: In undisturbed sedimentary rock, the lowest layers are the oldest.
fact: Sandstone is made of sand grains cemented together, often from old beaches, rivers or deserts.
fact: Limestone and chalk are made mostly from the shells and skeletons of tiny sea creatures.
fact: Shale and mudstone are made of very fine mud that settled in calm water, and they are the most common sedimentary rocks.
fact: Conglomerate is a rock made of rounded pebbles, rolled smooth by rivers or waves, cemented together.
fact: Most fossils are found in sedimentary rocks, because living things were buried in the layers.
fact: Metamorphic rocks are rocks changed by heat and pressure without melting.
fact: Marble is limestone that has been changed by heat and pressure into a rock of tightly packed crystals.
fact: Slate is shale changed by pressure so that it splits into thin, flat sheets, which is why it is used for roofs.
fact: Layers of rock can be bent into folds when plates collide.
fact: People have always built with local stone, so old towns often show the local geology, like the honey-coloured limestone of Bath or the granite of Aberdeen.
exercise: How do sedimentary rocks form? | Layers of sand, mud or shells are buried, pressed and cemented | Molten rock rises up, cools slowly and hardens into thick layers | Rock is changed by heat without melting | Crystals grow slowly in a cave from dripping water
exercise: In undisturbed layers of sedimentary rock, which layers are the oldest? | The lowest layers | The top layers in the sun | The middle layers of the pile | All layers are the same age
exercise: What is sandstone made of? | Sand grains cemented together | Tiny shells of sea creatures | Rounded pebbles | Melted lava
exercise: Where does the sand in sandstone often come from? | Old beaches, rivers or deserts | The inside of old volcanoes | The Earth's hot core | Melted glass from lightning strikes
exercise: What are limestone and chalk mostly made of? | Shells and skeletons of tiny sea creatures | Sand blown in from the deserts | Cooled lava from undersea volcanoes on the sea bed | Pressed leaves and wood
exercise: What are the most common sedimentary rocks? | Shale and mudstone | Marble and roofing slate | Granite and black basalt | Pumice and obsidian
exercise: Where did the mud of shale settle? | In calm water | On top of volcanoes | In fast mountain rivers | In the middle of the desert wind
exercise: What is conglomerate? | Rounded pebbles cemented together into rock | Rock made of fine mud from calm lakes | Natural glass from lava | Limestone changed by great heat and pressure
exercise: How do metamorphic rocks form? | Rocks are changed by heat and pressure without melting | Rocks melt completely and then cool again into crystals | Sand is cemented together by water | Shells pile up on the sea floor
exercise: What is marble made from? | Limestone changed by heat and pressure | Granite worn down by rivers into blocks | Sand pressed hard by the sea | Lava cooled quickly in cold water
exercise: What is slate made from? | Shale changed by pressure | Chalk changed by heavy rain | Basalt cooled slowly underground | Sandstone glued together with salt
exercise: Why is slate good for roofs? | It splits into thin, flat sheets | It floats on water | It is very soft and bends | It is see-through like glass
exercise: What can happen to rock layers when plates collide? | They can be bent into folds | They turn into lava at once | They become perfectly flat | They float up into the air
exercise: Why do old towns often show the local geology? | People built with the local stone | Towns were built only on volcanoes | Stone was always shipped from abroad | Geologists painted the houses
exercise: In which rocks are most fossils found? | Sedimentary rocks | Igneous rocks | Metamorphic rocks only | Lava flows
test: Josh says the top layer of a cliff must be the oldest because it's been there longest in the sun. Put him right. | In undisturbed layers the bottom ones were laid down first and are oldest | He's right, the top is always oldest because the sun has baked it longest | All the layers were made on the same day | The middle layer is always the oldest
test: "white cliffs of dover are made of what, like actual chalk??" | Yes, chalk made of shells of tiny sea creatures | No, they're made of white granite | No, they're painted limestone | Yes, chalk made of ground-up bones of dinosaurs
test: What would happen to a layer of limestone if it were buried very deep and heated without melting? | It could turn into marble | It would become sandstone | It would turn into chalk again | It would become basalt
test: A geologist finds a rock of rounded pebbles stuck together. What does the roundness tell her? | The pebbles were rolled by water, like in a river or on a beach | They were melted in a volcano and dripped into round blobs | They grew as crystals in a cave | They fell from space
test: In a cartoon, a statue of marble is shown being made by cooling lava in a mould. What's wrong? | Marble is changed limestone, not cooled lava | Nothing, that's how marble statues are made | Marble is made of pressed sand | Marble is made of hardened tree sap
test: "mate, why's every house in Bath the same yellow colour?" | They were built from the local honey-coloured limestone | The law makes everyone paint their house yellow | The sand from the beach stains them | The Romans covered them all in gold leaf and some of it stuck
test: Mia reckons metamorphic means the rock melted and set again. What's true? | Metamorphic rock changed with heat and pressure but did not melt | She's right, it melted completely | It means the rock was made by animals, like a coral reef | It means the rock came from space
test: Rock layers in a cliff are bent into a big arch. What most likely did that? | Plates colliding and squeezing the layers | Rain falling on the layers | Waves bending them last winter | The layers were laid down curved by wind
test: Why are you more likely to find a fossil fish in shale than in granite? | Shale formed from mud that buried living things; granite from melted rock | Fish only lived on top of granite | Granite fossils are too small to see | Shale is older than every other rock on Earth, so it has the most fossils
test: A roofer splits a stone into thin sheets with one tap. Which rock is it most likely to be? | Slate | Granite | Conglomerate | Pumice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of sedimentary rocks? | Particles are deposited in layers, buried, compacted and cemented | Molten rock is cooled and solidified at depth | Pre-existing rock is altered by heat and pressure without melting | Crystals are precipitated from volcanic gases
exam: Identify the rock which results from the metamorphism of limestone. | Marble | Hard slate | Granite | Conglomerate
exam: It is given that a sequence of sedimentary strata has remained undisturbed. Ascertain which statement concerning the relative age of the strata is correct. | The lowermost stratum is the oldest | The uppermost stratum is the oldest | All strata are of identical age | The age of the strata cannot be compared in any respect
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of chalk. | It consists principally of the shells and skeletons of minute marine organisms | It consists principally of cooled volcanic glass | It consists principally of compacted desert sand | It consists principally of the remains of terrestrial plants
